In this question, before seeing data:
blue ad click rate θb∼Beta(1,1). 
Red ad click rate 𝜃𝑟∼∼Beta(1,1).
     * uniform prior = Beta(1,1)” just means:
       we start with no bias about the click probability before data, treating all values in 
       [0,1] as equally likely.
Red ad click rate 𝜃𝑟∼∼Beta(1,1).
After observing:
30 clicks out of 100: 
posterior 𝜃𝑏∼Beta(30+1,70+1)=Beta(31,71).
40 clicks out of 100: 
posterior 𝜃𝑟∼Beta(41,61).
the fraction with θr > θb ≈ 0.90. (More draws → a more precise Monte Carlo estimate)
Interpretation:
Given 100 users per group:
Red had 40% clicks vs Blue 30%.
Bayesian analysis says there’s about a 90% probability that the red ad’s true click‐through rate is higher than blue’s.
Bayesian answer (with neutral priors Beta(1,1)):
Posterior probability that the red ad’s CTR exceeds the blue ad’s is ≈ 0.93.

Model: 
xb =30/100,xr=40/100 with 
𝜃𝑏∼Beta(31,71), 𝜃𝑟∼Beta(41,61)
Monte Carlo estimate of P(θr>θb) ≈ 0.93.
95% posterior credible interval for the difference θr−θb: ≈ [-0.033, 0.227].

Frequentist check (two-proportion z-test):
z≈1.48 → two-sided 
p≈0.14 (one-sided p≈0.07).
Not statistically significant at α=0.05.

Interpretation:
Given this data and neutral priors, there’s about a 93% chance the red ad’s true CTR is higher, but the evidence isn’t strong enough to pass a standard 5% frequentist significance test with only 100 users per arm.

