n_children	 0	      1	      2	   3	     4	    >=5
p	          0.3	     0.25	   0.2	0.15	  0.1	     0
Consider a random girl in the population of children. What is the probability that she has a sister?
Solution:
For families with 2 children (n=2, P(2)=0.2):
A girl has a sister if there are 2 girls in the family (k=2)
Probability of 2 girls in a family of 2: 
P(2 girls∣n=2)=
C(2, 2)*(0.5) ^2* (0.5) ^0
 =1×0.25=0.25.
Expected number of girls with sisters (2 girls or 2+ girls) from these families: 
k×P(k∣n)×P(n)=2×0.25×0.2=0.1

For families with 3 children (n=3, P(3)=0.15):
A girl has a sister if there are 2 girls in the family (k=2)
Probability of 2 girls in a family of 3: 
P(2 girls∣n=3)=
C(3, 2)*(0.5) ^2* (0.5) ^1
 =3×0.125=0.375
A girl has a sister if there are 2 girls in the family (k=3)
